So network-peer NAME resolution happens BEFORE `to == manager.selfId` (i.e. before "main" → parent) and before local child-name and fabric directory names. A network DM peer whose roster/DM sender label collides with:
- "main" (parent selfId display? "main" is the literal keyword),
- a local subagent name (e.g. "goal_builder"),
- a fabric directory session name,

...captures the address. The doc for `_resolveNetworkDmPeerAddress` says "Exact ids win; duplicate display names fail closed" — fail-closed among peers only, not vs local identities.

Mitigation: peer names come from `fromName` on inbound mail — which is the SENDER's roster label. A remote peer can label itself anything. The collision requires (a) a remote peer choosing exactly the local parent keyword "main" or an existing local sibling's display name, AND (b) the model addressing by bare name instead of the directory id. Directory listings show `name (id)` for peers and session names for mailboxes — ambiguity on the model side. Consequence: silently misdirected mail (goes to the remote peer over the encrypted transport instead of the local mailbox; no error).

